  "profile": "Bharatanatyam is a major Indian classical dance form originating in Tamil Nadu, recognized by the Sangeet Natak Akademi as one of India's oldest classical dance forms (Wikipedia). It is noted for a fixed upper torso, bent legs and flexed knees (aramandi), intricate footwork, and a vocabulary of hand, eye and facial gestures, accompanied by Carnatic music and a nattuvanar conductor (Wikipedia). Its repertoire spans nritta (pure dance), nritya (expression) and natya (drama). Once a temple dance known as Sadir/Sadiraattam, it was renamed Bharatanatyam around 1932 and revived for the public stage in the 20th century (ipassio).",
  "insight": "Rukmini Devi Arundale (with E. Krishna Iyer) popularized the name Bharatanatyam and, in January 1936, established the Kalakshetra academy at Adyar, Chennai, removing the erotic sringara elements of the Devadasi-era sadir style (Wikipedia).",
